  "notes": "West leads the ♣10 against 6NT. South has 9 top tricks, 3 spades, 2 hearts, the ♦A, and 3 clubs. If South can play diamonds for 1 loser he will have enough tricks for his contract.\n\nAfter winning West's club opening lead, suppose South begins by cashing the ♦A and leading dummy's small diamond. East follows small. It is correct to play the ♦Q. If diamonds are 3-3 the ♦Q and ♦10 are equally likely to work. If diamonds are 4-2, however, the only 4-2 break that can be picked up is West holding the ♦J doubleton.\n\nAlthough the ♦Q after the ♦A is better than a lower diamond, it still is not the proper way to play the suit.\n\nThe correct way to play diamonds is not intuitive. South should start by leading the ♦Q. When diamonds are 3-3 this play is just as good as cashing the ♦A and playing low to the ♦Q. One line will succeed when West has the ♦K third (with or without the ♦J). The other line will succeed when East has the same holdings.\n\nWhen diamonds are 4-2, however, leading the ♦Q is better. Leading the ♦Q picks up the doubleton ♦K with West. Cashing the ♦A first will not pick up that holding. On all other 4-2 breaks both lines of play work equally well.\n\nThe ♦Q play loses in 1 case that cashing the ♦A succeeds. That is when there is a singleton ♦K with East. That layout, however, is much less likely than West holding the ♦K doubleton.",
